    "q-61238": {
      "dom_id": "step-q61238",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(-2,2)$  and $(-2,7)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14179134/assets/39ac0f7718d7ee4ca8ed96b43c4f5ff9b644e0a3f7b5e2629052468b36ccf687.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14179134/assets/5995f7494a28a33cc4bdeef51d7d4cc0a85ee8e3219d8630d366c50345629d54.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14179134/assets/1cb541f6fcbb93b30a8f154763b0daa60e3b76142db1001ce804a2a68ba79247.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-2=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
